    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p70-q23-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Express the volume of the solid in terms of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p70-q23-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "V(x)=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}x(1-x)\\text{ m}^3",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", where ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "0<x<1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and x is measured in metres.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p70-q23-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use Pythagoras to find ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "AD^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then add the two cone volumes.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Take lengths in metres. The altitude of the isosceles triangle bisects its base, so $BD=DC=x$ and $AD\\perp BC$.\n\nFrom the perimeter,\n$$\\begin{align*}2AB+2x&=4\\\\AB&=2-x\\end{align*}$$\nBy Pythagoras in right triangle $ABD$,\n$$\\begin{align*}AD^2&=(2-x)^2-x^2\\\\AD^2&=4(1-x)\\end{align*}$$\nEach cone has radius $AD$ and height $x$. Using $V_{\\text{cone}}=\\frac13\\pi r^2h$,\n$$\\begin{align*}V(x)&=2\\times\\frac13\\pi\\bigl[4(1-x)\\bigr]x\\\\V(x)&=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}x(1-x)\\text{ m}^3\\end{align*}$$\nA positive base and altitude require $0<x<1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p70-q23-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the maximum possible volume.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p70-q23-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Maximum volume ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\text{ m}^3",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", attained at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac12\\text{ m}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p70-q23-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The downward-opening quadratic has its maximum halfway between its zeroes, 0 and 1.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The quadratic $V(x)=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}x(1-x)$ opens downwards and has zeroes at $x=0$ and $x=1$. Its maximum occurs halfway between the zeroes.\n$$\\begin{align*}x_{\\max}&=\\frac12\\\\V_{\\max}&=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\left(1-\\frac12\\right)\\\\V_{\\max}&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\text{ m}^3\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0<\\frac12<1$, this maximum is attainable.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
